A typical Football Bowl Subdivision team will normally employ a starting quarterback that has been around the block a time or two. Maybe it’s with another program, sitting on the bench for a season or two behind an older player, or has gotten to play for a while at a lower level school. Seldom do teams have a young, fresh-out-of-high-school, unproven young buck taking snaps under center.
Highly rated four-star and five-star quarterbacks coming out of prep football are the most likely to get this nod, especially in the era of the transfer portal. It is becoming few and far between where 18 and 19-year old young men are standing behind 350 pound offensive linemen with 100,000 screaming fans around them. Getting to play as a freshmen must mean that you’re good right? And that you will improve as time goes on?
That’s what we are going to try and figure out.
I went and looked at various different quarterback classes since the College Football Playoff had begun, in 2014-15, as that is when transfer portal activity really started to go on the uptick. I discovered that these freshmen quarterbacks, whether they were true or redshirt, were not getting a majority of the snaps. In some years, it was few and far between. Other years, it was the year of the child.
Let’s start by looking at the 2021 season, also known as the beginning of the Caleb Williams era. Williams, along with five other lower-tier FBS players, were the only six freshmen to start nine or more games for their respective schools in both their freshmen and sophomore seasons. This includes Williams at Oklahoma and USC, Seth Henigan at Memphis, Hayden Wolff at Old Dominion, Dequan Finn at Toledo, Daniel Richardson at Central Michigan, and Brady Olson at UMass.
I first set out to see if there was a correlation in improvement when it came to passer rating. You would think that there would be some growth in between Year 1 and Year 2, right? Well…


