So, we determined here that Caleb is good. Brady Olson still isn’t great. He actually wound up with more picks than scores. Ouch.
Henigan’s touchdown numbers went down a touch, but his interceptions didn’t go anywhere. Richardson threw less picks but had way less scores. Not exactly a shining star. The FBS average stayed about the same both seasons, around eight interceptions to 20 touchdowns.
Finn and Wolff saw some improvement. In Finn’s case, he was able to get his team into the end zone with the ball more. He also threw more than 70 additional passes his sophomore year, which led to the higher interception total. Wolff was the most drastic, though. His interceptions dropped and his touchdowns rose. Improvement! Old Dominion is on top of the world.
Okay, so there isn’t always noticeable improvement from Year 1 to Year 2. It is very dependent on the situation and the surrounding tools. NFL Draft QBs have to have done something right though? Right? Right?????

